WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:02.130
INTRO:
Have you ever wondered why social security

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.120
is absolute garbage?

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.330
Every year, they collect 12.4% of everybody’s
income up to $18,000.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:13.219
Yet, they’re projected to fall short of
their payouts by 2034.

00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:17.679
Some people blame it on the pandemic, others
blame it on bureaucracy, and others suggest

00:00:17.679 --> 00:00:21.699
that it’s simply a side effect of people
living longer than ever before.

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:25.480
While all of these are valid points, none
of these is the fundamental problem with social

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.480
security.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:31.890
You see, the fundamental problem with social
security is that it generates no real wealth.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.610
In 2021, Social Security pulled in $1.118
trillion in income.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:42.550
But $1.001 trillion of that was from contributions
and $41 billion was from income taxes charged

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.100
on benefits.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.940
Social security only generated $76 billion
in investment-based income which is a measly

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.550
2.61% annual return.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.470
Given that that barely keeps up with long-term
inflation if that, it’s no wonder why Social

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.070
Security is trash when it comes to creating
real wealth.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:02.830
Now, you don’t actually have to worry about
social security running out because I’m

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:06.460
sure they’re gonna either increase the tax
rate, decrease benefits, and/or print money

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:07.620
to make up the deficit.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.110
But, what if there’s a better solution.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.940
What if instead of investing in low-yielding
bonds, social security invested in the stock

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:18.260
market, real estate, and energy not just in
the US but internationally.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.090
This would generate true returns for social
security, and they would not be nearly as

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.160
dependent on contributions.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:29.430
At first glance, this might sound riskier
and it is, but this is exactly what sovereign

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.380
wealth funds around the world have been doing
for decades.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.290
And the most successful fund of them all is
Norway’s Sovereign Wealth Fund also known

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:38.010
as the Oil Fund.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:42.840
As of December 2021, the Norwegian Fund boasted
a total of $1.4 trillion worth of assets.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.479
To put that in perspective, Social Security
only controls $2.9 trillion despite the population

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:50.930
being 61 times larger.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:56.420
On a per-capita basis, Social Security has
$8,825 worth of assets per person.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:02.020
Meanwhile, Norway boasts a whopping $260,271
worth of assets per person.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.190
Norway literally has over a quarter-million
dollars worth of assets per person.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:09.860
Now, I’ve been picking on Social Security
thus far, but really, Norway blows the rest

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.150
of the world out of the water as well.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.659
China’s wealth fund, for example, controls
$1.1 trillion worth of net assets.

00:02:16.659 --> 00:02:20.379
But compared to their population, that’s
not even $1000 per person.

00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:26.489
So, here’s how Norway built the most successful
sovereign wealth fund in history.

00:02:26.489 --> 00:02:31.040
HISTORY:
Taking a look back, the history of Norway’s

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:45.409
wealth fund dates back to the year 1960 when
Norway’s prime minister Einar Gerhardsen

00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:47.190
got Norway involved in the oil business.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:51.650
He gave oil companies from around the world
licenses to come and search for oil within

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:52.650
Norway.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.370
Oil companies were allowed to keep the profits
they generated, but they had to pay petroleum

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:59.540
taxes and leases to Norway to continue operations.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:03.680
It took a couple of years to get the ball
rolling but starting in 1966, oil companies

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.739
flooded in looking for oil.

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:11.049
Within the next 4 years, they drilled 37 wells,
and on the day before Christmas Eve in 1969,

00:03:11.049 --> 00:03:14.689
they struck oil in the North Sea in an oil
field called Ekofisk.

00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:19.809
This turned out to be the largest oil field
ever found at sea, and by 1971, oil production

00:03:19.809 --> 00:03:21.029
would begin.

00:03:21.029 --> 00:03:24.620
It didn’t take long for Norway to start
raking in loads of money, and soon enough,

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.220
they started to discuss what to do with the
money.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:32.559
This was not a fast process by any means as
it would drag on for 12 years, but eventually,

00:03:32.559 --> 00:03:34.809
in 1983, they came to a consensus.

00:03:34.809 --> 00:03:38.499
They decided to create a fund that could invest
the profits generated from the oil industry

00:03:38.499 --> 00:03:40.519
hence the name oil fund.

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:44.379
The best part was that the government was
only allowed to spend real returns which are

00:03:44.379 --> 00:03:46.919
the returns after accounting for inflation.

00:03:46.919 --> 00:03:50.680
This seems like a no-brainer, but it’s not
nearly as common in application.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.859
We can’t give Norway too much praise though
given that their government was just as slow

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:56.560
as the rest of the world.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.120
It wasn’t till 1990 that Norway actually
went ahead and created the fund, and it wasn’t

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.609
till 1996 that the fund received its first
cash injection from the Ministry of Finance.

00:04:04.609 --> 00:04:10.079
It took 36 years to get to this point, but
at least they did it, and now, it was time

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:12.659
to invest.

00:04:12.659 --> 00:04:16.389
INVESTMENTS:
One of the hardest parts of running a successful

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:19.829
investment fund is effectively investing all
the capital.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.150
Even investing moguls like Warren Buffett
struggle with this issue.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.720
Norway also fell into this trap early on and
they would end up investing all of the money

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:27.960
into government bonds.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.520
But, fortunately, just one year later, they
diversified heavily into equities.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.880
Norway approved for 40% of the fund’s capital
to be invested into equities, and they gave

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.919
Norges Bank Investment Management the responsibility
of investing the money.

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:46.210
Within just 6 months, the bank converted all
40% into equities, and with that, they were

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:47.210
booming.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:51.180
In 2000, Norway approved the fund to diversify
into 5 emerging markets, so Norges had a pretty

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.650
broad set of choices when it came to investments.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.659
But this didn’t mean that they could just
invest in anything.

00:04:56.659 --> 00:05:00.849
There are 4 main categories that are blacklisted
from the fund for various reasons.

00:05:00.849 --> 00:05:04.689
First of all, the fund is not allowed to invest
in any weapon production companies like Boeing,

00:05:04.689 --> 00:05:06.730
Airbus, and Lockheed Martin.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:10.430
Secondly, the fund is not allowed to invest
in any tobacco companies.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:14.020
Thirdly, the fund is not allowed to invest
in any companies that cause environmental

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.099
damage.

00:05:15.099 --> 00:05:19.281
This one is a bit ironic given that all the
money is the fund originated from oil, but

00:05:19.281 --> 00:05:22.680
I guess they’re limiting their environmental
impact as much as possible.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.860
Anyway, the last major category the fund is
not allowed to invest in is companies that

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:28.650
are notorious for human rights violations.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:33.020
Fun fact, Walmart was blacklisted for this
reason till 2019.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:37.189
With all these guidelines, Norway was chugging
along through the 2000s quite strongly, but

00:05:37.189 --> 00:05:39.650
then the 2008 financial recession rolled around.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:46.139
In just one year, the fund lost 23.31% or
nearly a quarter of all their investments.

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:50.419
Many people in this situation would panic
and sell all their stocks, but Norway wasn’t

00:05:50.419 --> 00:05:51.419
concerned.

00:05:51.419 --> 00:05:55.680
In fact, they were excited and optimistic
to buy the dip.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.889
In 2007, Norway increased the fund’s allocation
to equities from 40% to 60%.

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:03.240
And in 2008, they created a 5% allocation
for real estate.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.949
Honestly, they couldn’t have played this
any better.

00:06:05.949 --> 00:06:10.000
They brilliantly bought up the bottom of the
market, and they have been rewarded extremely

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.000
well.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.800
In 2009, the fund posted a 25.6% return and
the fund has grown 5x since the recession.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:22.110
Today, the fund boasts an annualized return
of 6.56% since inception which is a bit lower

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.620
than the S&amp;P 500 7-8% annual.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:31.129
But, the Oil Fund is 18 times as diversified
as the S&amp;P 500 with stakes in 9,123 companies

00:06:31.129 --> 00:06:33.120
spread across 73 countries.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.169
At the end of every year, Norway reveals all
of its stakes in a massive 400-page document.

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:41.719
It would take forever to go through all these,
but just skimming over the most notable ones,

00:06:41.719 --> 00:06:46.861
they have $21 billion in Apple, $17 billion
in Microsoft, $7.8 billion in Facebook, and

00:06:46.861 --> 00:06:49.560
$14.5 billion in Amazon.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.720
Considering all this, I don’t think you’d
be surprised to hear that the Oil Fund is

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.570
the largest stock owner in Europe.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:58.360
And given how strategically they’ve played
the market so far, I think they more than

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.090
deserve that title.

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:05.349
WITHDRAWALS:
Having all that money is great and all, but

00:07:05.349 --> 00:07:07.580
what’s the point if you can’t spend it.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.210
As we previously touched on, Norway is allowed
to withdraw real returns from the fund every

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:16.159
year, but Norway didn’t even do this till
2016 when they made their first withdrawal.

00:07:16.159 --> 00:07:21.409
Since the 1990s, Norway has averaged about
2% inflation per year, so this would mean

00:07:21.409 --> 00:07:25.669
that Norway is allowed to withdraw about 4.5%
per year because that’s their real annual

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:26.669
return.

00:07:26.669 --> 00:07:28.509
But they don’t even allow for that.

00:07:28.509 --> 00:07:32.719
Norway limits its withdrawals to 3% per year
which is very similar to the 4% rule with

00:07:32.719 --> 00:07:34.169
the S&amp;P 500.

00:07:34.169 --> 00:07:38.680
The rule goes that if you withdraw 4% or less
every year from an investment into the S&amp;P

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.379
500, you’ll never run out of money.

00:07:40.379 --> 00:07:45.000
So, it’s again quite smart that Norway has
limited withdrawals to 3%.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.940
After all, you don’t want to kill the golden
goose.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:51.680
When the country needs money though, Norway
doesn’t hesitate to take out the full 3%.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:57.230
In 2020, for example, Norway withdrew $37
billion from the fund to battle the pandemic.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:01.960
Anyway, looking forward, Norway predicts that
the worst-case scenario for the fund in 2030

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:06.300
is $455 billion and that the best-case scenario
is $3.3 trillion.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:10.889
If the best-case scenario plays out, the Oil
Fund would overtake social security in assets

00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:12.949
in the second half of this decade.

00:08:12.949 --> 00:08:17.330
And at that rate, the fund would have $1 million
per citizen by 2040.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:21.280
Having said that, there’s no question that
the Oil Fund is a massive success financially.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:25.999
But, critics argue that the fund isn’t nearly
as successful when it comes to ethics.

00:08:25.999 --> 00:08:30.539
ETHICAL CONCERNS:
Probably the biggest criticism against the

00:08:30.539 --> 00:08:33.470
Oil Fund is that all of its contributions
are derived from oil.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:38.140
But the truth is if Norway didn’t take advantage
of the demand for oil, someone else would’ve

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:39.380
filled the gap.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:43.090
Over the past 60 years, it simply wasn’t
feasible to produce renewable energy on such

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:44.100
a large scale.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.820
So, if Norway avoided oil, it’s not like
customers would’ve switched to renewable

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:48.820
energy.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:52.370
They would’ve just gone to Saudi Arabia
or Canada for oil, and these countries have

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.250
plenty of oil to make up for the disparity.

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:58.560
So, personally, I don’t believe that Norway
did anything unethical by leveraging its resources

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.390
or the demand for oil.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:04.520
Another major criticism against the fund is
that their ethical boundaries aren’t enough.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.100
As a country, many argue that they have the
responsibility of being a good role model

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.370
and supporting productive businesses only.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:13.870
One of the industries that critics argue the
fund shouldn’t be involved in is gambling

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:15.070
and betting.

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:19.100
This has actually been an ongoing issue for
many years now, and Norway has taken steps

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.160
to ban investments in casinos.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:25.650
But so far, nothing concrete has taken place,
and the fund invested into Draftkings as recently

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.250
as March of 2021.

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:31.760
And finally, the third biggest criticism against
the fund is that it’s controlled by Norges

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:32.760
bank.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:36.420
Norges bank basically has full control of
the fund, and as long as they don’t break

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.780
any guidelines, they can do whatever they
want.

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:43.480
The bank does have 500 well-qualified financial
professionals, but at the end of the day,

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.880
final investment decisions are left to just
17 finance ministry bureaucrats.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:51.500
When the fund had less than $100 billion,
this wasn’t a very big concern.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:55.070
But now that the fund is reaching into the
trillions, these guys have substantial financial

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:56.070
power.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:59.980
They could use this power to prop up their
own investments and/or manipulate the markets.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:03.600
Aside from having a lot of power, many argue
that these leaders aren’t actually that

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.650
great when it comes to investing and that
hiring better leaders would lead to far better

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:08.650
returns.

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:12.140
While all of these points are fair, I don’t
think you can complain too much given how

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:15.310
well the fund is performing in comparison
to the rest of the world.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:20.160
APPLICATION TO THE US:
Given how successful the sovereign wealth

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:23.900
fund has been for Norway, would it be possible
to apply such a system to the US?

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:28.210
Well, some states in the US already have a
sovereign wealth fund like Alaska.

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:32.710
The Alaska Permanent Fund was created back
in 1976, and similar to the Oil Fund, the

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:35.150
Permanent Fund is made possible through oil
revenue.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:40.830
As of 2021, the fund had $81.9 billion worth
of assets, and they disperse roughly $1600

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:41.830
per year per resident.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:46.710
So, clearly, such a concept is not foreign
to the US; however, it would be quite difficult

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:50.420
to create a national sovereign fund especially
if it was funded using contributions.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:55.080
Here’s the thing, while such a program would
be extremely helpful to people with bad spending

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:59.210
habits and little to no investments, it’s
not all that helpful for disciplined savers

00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:00.210
and investors.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:04.540
At the end of the day, such a fund is just
going to match the market if that.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:07.050
So, many would far prefer to just do that
themselves.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:11.990
If such a fund was created though, it wouldn’t
be all that hard to manage logistically.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.580
For example, if half of Social Securities’
reserves were converted into a wealth fund,

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:17.990
that would entail investing about $1.5 trillion.

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:22.570
While that’s a large amount, it’s quite
manageable within the financial world.

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:25.810
After all, BlackRock has nearly $10 trillion
under management.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:29.750
So, creating such a fund would definitely
be more of a political issue than a logistical

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:30.750
issue.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:34.750
Personally, I’d be a fan of converting Social
Security into a wealth fund given that any

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:38.060
sort of return would be better than Social
Security’s 0% real returns.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.010
But that’s just what I think.

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.010
Would you guys support a sovereign wealth
fund?

